1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hock How came to America with his father on October 27, 1915. Before leaving China he married NG Chau Hai in April 1915. He was kept on Angel Island until February 23, 1916 when he was finally admitted to the U.S. 
2 CONC as the son of a native. Hock How's wife NG Chau Hai died unexpectedly from a heart attack in 1918. He attended Palo Alto High School where he learned English, and was admitted to Stanford University where he studied electrical eng
2 CONC ineering. During this time he worked as a houseboy to pay his way through school.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1924, he took a camping trip through the Northwestern U.S. and Canada.  In 1926, he returned to China for the first time and married CHU Tui Goon on May 1 of that year. Hock How and Tui Goon had their first son Larry in 1928. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hock How returned to the U.S. in 1928 to continue his studies and completed his master’s degree in 1932.  In the midst of the great depression, there were few job opportunities for Chinese, let alone college educated ones, in Amer
2 CONC ica.  So, Hock How returned to China after graduation where he became a teacher.  Hock How’s second son, Jack, was born in 1933.  The family moved to Canton, where Paul and Lily were born, then to Hong Kong in 1938. In July 1934, 
2 CONC Hock How joined the new established Institute of Technology (工學院) at Sun Yat Sen University (中山大學) as a professor.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the war, Hock How moved the family to Liuchou from 1941-1942 then to Kunming from 1942-1947, where Mary was born in 1943. As a US educated engineer, Hock How was commissioned by Chiang Kai Shek and the US Army to help build
2 CONC  the Burma Road.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family then travel down the Burma road from Kunming to Rangoon where the lived for several years. In 1946, he brought  Larry to New York. He brought Jack and Paul to San Francisco in 1949, and Tui Goon followed with Lily and M
2 CONC ary in 1954.  Meanwhile, Hock How stayed in Burma importing Packard automobiles and other American goods.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hock How moved to Hong Kong in 1960 running his businesses in Burma from there.  In 1964, the Burmese Government turned fully socialist, nationalizing all companies, and the family’s fortune was wiped out.  At a time when he shoul
2 CONC d have been looking forward to retirement, Hock How now struggled to make ends meet.
2 CONT
2 CONT He branched out to other SE Asia countries, and by 1967, his company, The China Engineers (Thailand) Ltd, represented American exporters, such as John Deere Industrial tractors, Ingersoll-Rand air compressors, American Hoist Co., 
2 CONC Universal Engineering Co. and others.  With keen competition, the company did not turn a profit until 1968.
2 CONT
2 CONT That same year, Hock How returned to live in Hong Kong full-time, and in 1969, he joined the import export firm, Deacon & Co. Ltd, as manager of their Import Department.  He wrote that “[my work] is much easier than my work in Ban
2 CONC gkok where my responsibility was much greater with company financial worries.  Here my work consists of writing a few letters a day, calculating a few figures, costs of goods and pepping up the salesmen with encouraging remarks, e
2 CONC tc.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Hock How maintained a vigorous lifestyle, waking at 6am and walking to the park every day.    “That gives me a lot of pep for the day,” he once wrote.  In his mid-seventies, he commented that he felt younger everyday and that many
2 CONC  people said he looked like 55.  He often wrote fondly about taking the three hour steamer ride to Macau to spend a quiet week or weekend.
2 CONT
2 CONT He continued to work and live in Hong Kong until his death in 1979.
