1 OCCU Certified Public Accountant
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jack Lan Hong (曾連卓) was born in China, Guangdong Province, Toishan County, Ong On Village, (中國廣東省台山縣東安村) on October 15, 1933. He was the second son of Hong Hock How was a Stanford trained electrical engineer, educa
2 CONC tor, and business man and Tui Goon Chu Hong. Shortly after he was born, he, his parents and older brother Larry moved to Guangzhou, where his brother Paul and sister Lily was born. During China’s war with Japan, the family moved f
2 CONC requently ending up in Kunming, China, where his youngest sister Mary was born and Jack acquired his taste for spicy food. During this time they also lived with an older cousin, Lan Ngin, who they considered to be their eldest bro
2 CONC ther. After the war, the family moved to Rangoon, Burma, where the children attended the Wah Sha School. Jack had a gift for the Chinese language and by this time spoke four dialects: Cantonese, Toisan, Kunming, and Mandarin.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1949, Jack immigrated to America with his brother Paul as the sons and grandsons of United States citizens. The two brothers lived in Chinatown. In 1952 Jack graduated from Commerce High School, where he was a championship swim
2 CONC mer.  Jack attended the City College of San Francisco and was the 1953 Chinese Club president. After City College, Jack served in the United States Army as a medic and interpreter from 1955 to 1957. He was stationed at Fort Ord in
2 CONC  Monterey, CA and Fort Sam Houston in San Antonio, TX before being posted along the DMZ in South Korea. After being discharged from the Army, Jack earned his bachelor's degree from San Francisco State in 1959.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jack’s first job was working for Continental Insurance selling plans to brokers.  In his first year, Jack won an award for being one of the companies top sellers. In spite of his success, Jack felt that a career selling insurance 
2 CONC was not for him and joined the State of California as a savings and loan auditor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 18, 1962, Jack married Rose Sen Chin of Oakland, CA at St. Paulus Lutheran Church in San Francisco, CA.  They moved to San Mateo, CA in 1964, and their first child, Carole Lisa, was born on May 11, 1965, followed by their
2 CONC  son, Kenneth Jeffrey, on October 30, 1967.
2 CONT
2 CONT During this time, Jack decided that the best way to provide for his family would be to have the option to “hang up his own shingle”, and started to study to be a Certified Public Accountant. While continuing to work for the State,
2 CONC  Jack built a successful public accounting practice, and went on to obtain real estate broker and financial planning licenses to round out his offerings. When Jack retired after 33 years of service with the State of California in 
2 CONC 1996, he was the Chief of Audits for the Department of Health Services’ Northern California Division and had attained the highest rank possible for a non-political appointee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although his work often took him away from home, Jack used his time productively. He stayed fit by playing tennis or jogging with his fellow auditors, after which he would retire to his hotel room to study. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At home he was just as busy keeping up with family and friends. Weekends were time for family, and without fail, Jack would take Rose and the kids to his mother’s for dinner on Saturday, and Rose’s mother’s on Sunday. He would att
2 CONC end weekend sports events for Carole and Kenny, first swimming, then soccer and basketball. Jack also enjoyed family ski trips to Lake Tahoe where he and his sister’s had chipped into to buy a cabin. When his children were in high
2 CONC  school, Jack also served as advisor for the Foster City Chinese Youth Group. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jack had other passions as well. He loved to cook and he was famous for his Char Sui Ribs and his “always too hot for everyone else” curry chicken. Strong and athletic Jack enjoyed tennis, skiing, and golf. Jack also enjoyed rough
2 CONC ing it on week-long hunting and fishing trips with his brother-in-laws Gene and King and cousin Lin. He was a world traveller and throughout the 1980’s acted as a tour guide for friends and family travelling back to China. Jack pu
2 CONC rsued these activities through the golden years of his retirement.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the last seven years even as his body was slowing down, Jack’s appetite for life never did. He still traveled albeit with his wheelchair and scooter. He still cooked, and when he couldn’t do that he enjoy going out to his f
2 CONC avorite Chinese restaurants with family and friends. He attend continuing education classes and CPA society meetings. He loved to watch his grandkids playing ball. He loved to getting together with family even when the short ride 
2 CONC there and back exhausted him. At his nursing home, Jack, ever the social butterfly, knew everyone, and communicated any way he could with the other residents even if it was with a just wave and a smile.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note on Generation Numbers (140, 128, 75, 71, 18世)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Jack is a 140th generation descendant of Huang Di (the Yellow Emperor), and following the same line of descent:
2 CONT
2 CONT * a 128th generation descendant of Céng Qū Liè 鄫曲烈, 13, 1世, who was the first to the name Céng
2 CONT * a 75th generation descendant of Zēng Wū 曾巫, 66, 54, 1世, who was the first to use the name Zēng
2 CONT * a 71st generation descendant of the Confucian disciple, Zēng Shēn 曾參, 70, 58, 5, 1世
2 CONT * a 18th generation descendant of Zēng Zi Róng 曾子榮 123, 111, 58, 54, 1世 who establish the branch of the family in Tai Shan County, Guangdong, China
2 CONT * a grandson of Hong Chew Yook 曾稠毓 138, 126, 73, 69, 16世, who was the first member of his family to live in the United States and adopted the surname Hong in English
