2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 辛亥革命先驱丘明昶
2 CONT Xinhai Revolutionary Pioneer Khoo Beng Cheang
2 CONT
2 CONT The Xinhai Revolution led by Dr Sun Yat-Sen is the first great capitalist-bourgeois-democratic revolution in China’s history. The revolution overthrew the China’s two thousand years old feudal system and established a democratic r
2 CONC epublic. The historical significance of the Xinhai Revolution is bound to shine through the ages. Overseas Chineses actively participated in the revolution process, amongst them was Mr Khoo Beng Cheang who hailed from Xin-An villa
2 CONC ge. Khoo Beng Cheang was a crucial figure in the headquarters of the Nanyang Tongmenghui. He worked together with Dr Sun Yat-Sen and made important contributions to the revolution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Providing Strong Support for the Xinhai Revolution
2 CONT At a young age, Khoo left Xin-An village and travelled to Nanyang. He has been to Singapore and Malaysia and eventually settled down in Penang. Through his dilligence and intelligence, he became a successful businessman. When Sun 
2 CONC Yat-Sen first visited Penang in 1905, he was warmly received by Khoo Beng Cheang and a few other prominent overseas Chineses. Sun gave an important speech at the Xiao Lan Ting Club（小兰亭）, where he pointed out that the corrupted Qin
2 CONC g Government was the root cause for China’s plight of being underdeveloped and bullied by the Western nations. In order to save the country, the Qing Government must be overthrown. Khoo, who was forward-thinking and full of righte
2 CONC ousness and idealism, found an epiphany from Sun’s speech. He was deeply impressed and became a staunch supporter of Sun’s ideals of “reinvigorating the Chinese nation”.
2 CONT Khoo Beng Cheang was one of the earliest members to join the Tongmenghui when Sun Yat-Sen established a branch in Penang. He was also one of the first overseas Chinese from Gulang Island of Xiamen to join the Tongmenghui. Khoo was
2 CONC  an important assistant to Sun’s revolutionary cause. He actively recruited members into the Tongmenghui and helped the organisation to grow. In fact, Khoo was one of the three persons who inducted former Chairman of the All-China
2 CONC  Federation of Returned Overseas Chinese, Mr Chng Hee Chuan into the Tongmenghui.
2 CONT Khoo Beng Cheang was also a Deputy Director（协理）of the Penang Chinese Town Hall （平章会馆）. When Sun Yat-Sen visited Penang again in 1907, Khoo mobilised the Chinese community to listen to Sun’s speech. During the speech, Sun stated th
2 CONC at in order to save China, it was necessary to overthrow the Manchurians first. Khoo was dogged in his support to Sun’s revolutionary cause and did not waver because of the resistance put forth by the “Royalist” .
2 CONT In 1908, Sun visited Penang again. Again, he was warmly received by Khoo and other compatriots from the Tongmenghui. He also gave another speech at the Xiao Lan Ting Club. This time, Sun asserted, “If the Manchurians do not go, Ch
2 CONC ina will be doomed.” Khoo cooperated with Sun by rallying the local Chinese community to participate in the Anti-Qing revolution.
2 CONT In 1910, Sun was invited to give another speech at the Chinese Merchants’ Club (清芳阁俱乐部). Seeing that the Chinese Merchants’ Club is the place where big capitalists and businessmen in Penang often frequent, Khoo seized the opportun
2 CONC ity by accompanying Sun to the Chinese Merchants’ Club and helped to rally support for the revolution. During the speech, Sun said, “For their own benefits, overseas Chinese ought to first help China become great again. Only when 
2 CONC China is strong and rich, would the interests of the overseas Chinese receive sturdy governmental protection.” Sun’s perspectives were well-received by most of the overseas Chinese who were at the event.
2 CONT Despite the fact that the anti-Qing uprisings led by Sun failed one after another, for instance the Huanggang Uprising in Teochew, the Hekou Uprising in Yunnan, the Zhennanguan Uprising and the Gengxu New Army Uprising in Guangzho
2 CONC u, Khoo remained steadfast in supporting Sun and his revolutionary ideals.
2 CONT As these military uprisings required copious amount of funding, ammunitions and weapons, Khoo donated large amount of his own money to support the revolution. He also went around to lobby for donations from the local Chinese commu
2 CONC nity. Khoo’s generosity and efforts helped to financially support the revolution.
2 CONT In consideration of the geographical conditions of Penang, the overseas Chinese and the core cadres of Tongmenhui, Sun Yat-Sen decided to move the headquarters of the Nanyang Tongmenghui from Singapore to Penang. Khoo became a cor
2 CONC e figure of the headquarters. It was also at the Penang headquarters where most of the plans for the revolution to overthrow the Qing Government were made.
2 CONT In 13 November 1910, Sun chaired the historical Penang Conference（庇能会议）in Penang, where Khoo particpated in the planning of the earthshaking “Yellow Flower Mound Uprising in Guangzhou” of 1911. This particular uprising shook the Q
2 CONC ing Government’s rule over China and ostensibly expanded the influence of the revolutionaries. After the Guangzhou Uprising, people in China became more passionate in the revolution and in overthrowing the Manchurians. The Guangzh
2 CONC ou Uprising marked the start of the Xinhai Revolution and it paved the way for the success of the subsequent Wuchang Uprising.
2 CONT In  April 1911, the Guangzhou Uprising fell apart. Sun returned from America and sought asylum in Penang, as he was rejected entry by the authorities in Japan,Vietnam and Singapore. Khoo Beng Cheang and his fellow comrades sprung 
2 CONC to Sun’s assistance. They helped Sun and his family to settle down at 404 Dato' Kramat Road. When Sun ran into financial difficulties, Khoo Beng Cheang and ten other overseas Chineses helped to take care of all the Sun Family’s li
2 CONC ving expenses. Khoo played an important role in helping Sun surmount a difficult period in his life.
2 CONT After the revolutionaries took control of Xiamen, the provisional government set up by the revolutionaries was faced with financial problems. Khoo and other overseas Chinese from Penang immediately helped to raise funds and send m
2 CONC oney to Xiamen. They also helped to set up a “suicide squad” to join the revolution. After the Wuchang Uprising, the provisional government ran into financial difficulties as well. As a result, Sun Yat-Sen set up the Chinese Indus
2 CONC trial Bank and searched for financiers from Southeast Asia. Despite the fact that the colonial government disallowed overseas Chinese from remitting money back to support the revolution, members of the Penang Tongmenghui used all 
2 CONC sorts of means to circumvent the proscription. Khoo was amongst the seven overseas Chinese who raised the most amount of funds for the provisional government, along with revolutionary pioneer Tan Sin Cheng.
2 CONT In 1914, when the revolutionary army required funding for its military expenses, Khoo defied the prohibitions set by the colonial government and went ahead with fund-raising for the revolutionary army. Khoo Beng Cheang once said, 
2 CONC “A real man ought to fly like an eagle, when the opportunity arises, he should immediately charge forward.” Khoo’s bold attitude was highly praised and respected by Sun Yat-Sen.
2 CONT In 12 December 1911, when Sun Yat-Sen returned to China from America, he made a detour to visit his family members in Penang. During this visit, Sun also discussed national affairs with Khoo and a few others. Sun invited Khoo to f
2 CONC ollow him back to China and help with governing the country. However, Khoo saw that his job was done and did not want to receive further glory.
2 CONT After the Republic of China was established, Sun, who became the president, wrote a letter to Khoo. In his letter, Sun sought Khoo’s for advice in terms of running the country and expressed his wish for Khoo to return to China and
2 CONC  assume a governmental position. However, Khoo Beng Cheang politely declined Sun’s offer. According to Khoo, his ambition was to serve the country, and was not interested in gold and glory.
2 CONT In order to recognise Khoo for his valuable contributions towards the revolution, Sun Yat-Sen awarded him with a Letter of Appreciation. The headquarters of the Nanyang Tongmenghui also gave Khoo a pair of bullet shells used in th
2 CONC e Nanchang Uprising as souvenirs. The two bullet shells were engraved with the words “For Khoo Beng Cheang of the Nanyang Tongmenghui Headquarter to keep” and “In commemoration of the founding of the Republic of China; Day 1 Month
2 CONC  1 Year 1 of the Republic”. （“南洋同盟会总机关丘明昶先生惠存”；“建设民国纪念，民国元年元月元日”）
2 CONT After Khoo passed away, his gravestone was placed in a direction that faced China. It was inscribed with the words: “Beneath this grave lies a soul which still loves his country” （羁魂犹恋国）. The comrades of the Tongmenghui regretted 
2 CONC Khoo’s passing and held a grand memorial service for him. His tombstone was engraved with the revolutionary army banner that was personally designed by Sun Yat-Sen and inscribed with a recital of Khoo’s contributions to the revolu
2 CONC tion. There was also an elegiac couplet on the tombstone that reads: “Beneath these green hills lies the body of a chivalrous man and the surrounding earth mourns for his heroic soul”（青山埋侠骨，黄土吊英魂）. Khoo’s name and his deeds were a
2 CONC lso recorded in the first volume of the “Illustrious Chinese of Nanyang”《南洋名人集传》 series. His contributions to the Xinhai Revolution were also recorded in “Revolutionaries of Nanyang”《南洋革命英烈传》. Not only would these recordings of Kh
2 CONC oo Beng Cheang’s life stories help the younger generation to remember his contributions, but it also served as a medium to pass on the patriotic spirit of our revolutionary pioneers.
2 CONT Khoo’s deep love for his country and his unwavering support for the revolution, at the expense of sacrificing his personal wealth, time and effort, was highly recognised by the Chinese government. In April 2011, Premier Wen Jiabao
2 CONC  visited Malaysia and met the descendants of several revolutionary pioneers. The Chinese Embassy extended a special invitation to Dr Khoo Teng Cheok, grandson of Khoo Beng Cheang, to meet Premier Wen in Kuala Lumpur. China’s CCTV 
2 CONC broadcasted the meeting where Khoo Teng Cheok shared an intimate conversation with Premier Wen. According to Khoo Teng Cheok, as a descendant of a revolutionary, he was touched and excited at the same time, to receive concern from
2 CONC  Premier Wen. Khoo Teng Cheok expressed that he would carry forward Sun Yat-Sen’s revolutionary ideals and try his best to contribute to the “reinvigoration of the Chinese nation”. Khoo Teng Cheok also expressed well wishes for Ch
2 CONC ina to become stronger and richer.
2 CONT Setting up Revolutionary Newspapers and the Penang Philomatic Union
2 CONT Sun Yat-Sen believed that in order for the revolution to be successful, public opinion must be shaped to support the revolution. Hence, establishing a newspaper to criticise and expose the corruption of the Qing Government was inc
2 CONC umbent. The newspaper could also propagate democratic and revolutionary ideals to help China become great again. Sun Yat-Sen personally came up with the name for Kwong Wah Jit Poh, which symbolised “bringing glory back to China”. 
2 CONC The setting up of Kwong Wah Jit Poh was left to Tan Sin Cheng, Khoo Beng Cheang and four other Chineses in Penang. The first issue of the Kwong Wah Jit Poh was formally published in December 1910, making it Penang’s first revoluti
2 CONC onary newspaper. The Kwong Wah Jit Poh played an important role in rallying suport and crowdfunding for the revolution. After serving as the Councilor（议员）of the Kwong Wah Jit Poh in 1911, Khoo Beng Cheang assumed other senior posi
2 CONC tions such as General Manager, Director and Chairman for the next twenty odd years. He had many contributions to the newspaper, in terms of both advice and funding. These contributions could still be found in the records of Kwong 
2 CONC Wah Jit Poh.
2 CONT In 1912, Khoo Beng Cheang also became a committee member of the “Pacific Daily News” (太平洋日报) and the director of Singapore’s Kuo Min Yit Poh. In 1916, he became a committee member of the “Xiamen Minzhong Newspaper” (厦门民钟报). These 
2 CONC newspapers were possible because of the devotion and effort of revolutionaries like Khoo Beng Cheang and they  htremendously helped the revolution.
2 CONT During that period of time, the Tongmenghui was an underground organisation in British Malaya. In order to implement revolutionary activties, Khoo Beng Cheang along with Goh Say Eng and several others, established the Penang Philo
2 CONC matic Union to serve as a cover for the Tongmenghui to exceute revolutionary activities. Since its inception in 1909, Khoo assumed important positions in the Penang Philomatic Union for a total of 30 years. Khoo was the Union’s As
2 CONC sistant Manager (协理)，Union Chief, Vice-Union Chief, Senator(评议员), Inspector (监察员), Vice-Chairperson and Chairperson. After the founding of the Republic of China, Sun Yat-Sen issued an Letter of Appreciation (“旌义状”) to the Penang P
2 CONC hilomatic Union.
2 CONT Promoting the Chinese Culture, Building Schools and Nurturing Talents
2 CONT As early as 1904, Khoo Beng Cheang had been involved in the setting up of the first Chinese school in Malaya – the Penang Chung Hwa School. The founders of the school were forward-thinking and the school’s curriculum consisted of 
2 CONC knowledge from both China and the West. As such, the Penang Chung Hwa School was also one of first school in Malaya to embrace modern education.
2 CONT The colonial government of the day put forth many restriction on Chinese education. This situation triggered Khoo’s nationalist pride, making him more aware of the importance of Chinese education. As such, he collaborated with Tan
2 CONC  Sin Cheng to found the Chung Ling School in 1917.
2 CONT In 1934, he helped Chung Ling School to build dormitory for secondary school students. Today, at the main gate of Chung Ling High School, there is still a foundation stone with the words:“Laid by Khoo Beng Cheang” (丘明昶奠基) inscribe
2 CONC d on it. Since 1918, Khoo had assumed important roles in the Chung Ling School, such as General Manager and Chairperson. He was appointed as Honarary Principal of the school from 1934 to 1938. For more than twenty years, he devote
2 CONC d much energy and passion towards nurturing Chinese talents in the capacity of an overseas Chinese.
2 CONT Khoo Beng Cheang was also very much against the prevalent gender inequality perceptions within the Chinese society. In 1919, he partnered a few other members of the Penang Philomatic Union to found the Fukien Girls’ School (now kn
2 CONC own as Penang Chinese Girls' High School). Khoo was subsequently elected to be the schools’ assistant manager (协理) and director. For several decades, these two schools – Chung Ling and Fukien Girls’ School – nurtured countless pat
2 CONC riotic youths. Today, the two schools continue to enjoy a good reputation amongst the people in Penang.
2 CONT Supporting Anti-Japanese Movements; Providing Disaster Reliefs 
2 CONT After the “September 18 Incident” of 1931, the Japanese army invaded Shenyang a city in northeast China, and in 1932, the Japanese invaded Shanghai. Khoo was very indignant after knowing that China and its people are being harmed 
2 CONC by the Japanese army. During the same period of time, China was also hit by severe natural disasters such as floods, droughts and typhoons, and harvest were poor. As a result of these internal and external calamities, the liveliho
2 CONC od of many people in China were severely affected. Khoo took it upon himself to help the people in China. After visiting China to have a sense of the situation, he immediately contacted fellow revolutionary comrades and began sour
2 CONC cing for donations. Khoo became the chairman of Overseas Chinese Relief Fund Committee (华侨筹赈伤兵难民委员会) where he actively mobilised the local Chinese community to send money back to China to support the war against Japan and provide 
2 CONC disaster reliefs. He was also the chairperson of Freshman Spirit Encouragement Society (新生励志社) and China Bank of Domestic Good Pennang IPO Committee (中国国货银行槟城募股委员会). He spearheaded the movement to boycott Japanese goods and galvan
2 CONC ized the locals to use products from China instead. To a certain extent, this helped to promote the growth of China’s manufacturing industry. He also incessantly participated in fundraising activities for Xiamen and Nanjing. He sp
2 CONC ared no effort in trying to help China, and never did he complain.
2 CONT Setting up businesses; Venturing into the finance industry of Singapore and Malaysia
2 CONT After arriving in Nanyang, Khoo Beng Cheang set up a business selling ropes, “Keat Cheang”, in Penang. As his business grew, “Keat Cheang” expanded into Indonesia and Singapore. Subsequently, Khoo also opened a rice mill and grew 
2 CONC rubber.
2 CONT In addition, Khoo also ventured into the finance industry. He was one of the founders of Singapore’s Ho Hong Bank and simultaneously served as the presidents and directors of Ho Hong Bank and Oversea-Chinese Bank for sixteen years
2 CONC . In 1935, Khoo was one of the financiers of the Singapore United Overseas Bank and was elected to be Vice-President of the bank. In the same year, he assumed the role of treasurer at the Chinese Chamber of Commerce, making him on
2 CONC e of the pioneers of Singapore and Malaysia’s overseas Chinese finance industry. Khoo contributed greatly to the development of Nanyang’s finance industry.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Physically in Nanyang, Emotionally Attached to his Hometown; Khoo contributed greatly to Public Welfare in Xiamen
2 CONT Khoo maintained deep emotional relations with his hometown. When he returned to Xin-An village and saw that the village was in a state of decline, he expressed that he “could not shirk away the responsibility” of rejuvenating his 
2 CONC hometown. In 1922, he wrote a letter to the village elders of Xin-An and gave suggestions such as removing the public toilets beside the roads, building new roads, clearing the drains, ameliorating public hygiene, and improving pu
2 CONC blic facilities. He also expressed his disapproval against wasting money on organising operas that were depicting feudalistic ideas and harmful to moral standards. In addition, Khoo proposed to put a stop to all superstitious acti
2 CONC vities. Khoo stated that peace and wealth of the people and the country had got nothing to do with gods or deities. He set an example by removing every objects that are linked to rituals or superstition in his own house.
2 CONT Khoo also advocated for women’s rights. He was against the custom of foot-binding in China. Khoo pointed out, “This world is about the survival of the fittest, women are the mothers of our children, if our women are uneducated, we
2 CONC  would not have wise mothers, and this implies that we would not have good descendants.” He strongly advocated for girls’ schools to be given equal resources and attention as boys’ schools in the village. In his letter, he emphasi
2 CONC sed that ensuring the survival of the girls’ school in the village was of the utmost importance to the village. Despite the fact that the economy in Nanyang was bad during that period of time, Khoo personally brought money back to
2 CONC  Xin-An to ensure that the girls’ school would not be closed down due to the lack of funds. As a result of Khoo’s goodwill, the girls in Xin-An were able to receive free education, books and stationery.
2 CONT Khoo’s contribution to his hometown and his generosity towards the funding of schools were deeply appreciated and respected by the people of Xin-An village.
2 CONT Khoo also saw to the maintenance of the red-bricked old houses in Xin-An. In addition, he built new houses in Gulang Island of Xiamen as well. Two years ago, when the delegation from Malaysia’s Sun Yat-Sen Research Center came to 
2 CONC Xiamen, they specially visited Khoo Beng Cheang’s former residence at No. 74 Huangyan Road of Gulangyu. According to the delegation, Khoo Beng Cheang played an important role in the Xinhai Revolution. The house at No. 74 should be
2 CONC  preserved as it signifies the support rendered by overseas Chinese during the Xinhai Revolution.
2 CONT Khoo’s next door neighbour was Hui Bon Hoa (黄仲训) . Khoo was involved in a lawsuit where he sued Hui Bon Hoa for attempting to monopolise the Sunlight Rock district (日光岩). Hui Bon Hoa intended to convert the scenic area into a priv
2 CONC ate garden, while Khoo suggested to build a public park on the piece of land.
2 CONT Khoo also participated in the funding of the Zhongshan Park, Zhongshan Hospital, Gulang Island Zhongshan Library. He also served on the board of directors for the Gulang Island Zhongshan Library. In the library, there was a room n
2 CONC amed after him (Beng Cheang Room) to recognise his donations. Khoo also donated generously in schools and city facilities. He set up the “Beng Cheang Company”, a trading firm, along Xiamen’s Kaiyuan Road, contributing to the city’
2 CONC s then emerging import-export industry. In 1928, Khoo remitted 14,000 da yuan (the currency of Republic of China then) to buy a 220 square feet piece of land along Dou Zai Wei Road of Xiamen and built a factory there. His aim was 
2 CONC to stimulate the growth of Xiamen’s manufacturing industry and reinvigorate China’s economic development by bringing in high-end equipment from abroad. When Xiamen was invaded by the Japanese, Khoo’s company continuously shipped c
2 CONC ommodities such as rice and lumber and supplied them to the local people, depsite all odds. Khoo spared no effort in doing good for the country and the people. This, in return, earned him a good reputation amongst the people in Ch
2 CONC ina and the overseas Chinese community.
