1 NAME Keng Kiat /LEE/
2 GIVN Keng Kiat
2 SURN LEE
2 _MARNM LEE
2 NICK Lee Keng Keat
1 NAME LEE Keng Kiat
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 22 JAN 1851
2 ADDR
3 STAE Malacca
3 CTRY Malaysia
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 FEB 1917
2 PLAC 81 Amoy Street
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Singapore
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mr Lee Keng Kiat was the son of Mr Lee Quee Lim and was born in Malacca in 1851 and educated in St. Xavier's Institution, Penang. For many years, Lee Keng Kiat was associated with the firm Keng Yong Brothers and la
2 CONC ter joined Straits Steamship Co has its sub-manager until his retirement in 1911. Lee Keng Kiat had the honor of representing the Chinese merchant community in congratulating the Sultan of Johore on July 8, 1886 on the assumption 
2 CONC of his title as Sultan, has been recognised by Queen Victoria The address was read in English by Lee Keng Kiat and contained more than 150 signatures. In 1900, he was elected as a member of the committee of the Straits Chinese Bri
2 CONC tish Association, the year of the associations formation. His intense patriotism was demonstrated in 1901, when at the age of 50, he enlisted as one of the original members of the Singapore Volunteer Infantry (Chinese Company). (S
2 CONC .V.C).
2 CONT
2 CONT  Lee Keng Kiat passed away on February 9, 1917 at the age of 6 at 81 Amoy Street, leaving behind 5 sons. Two of them, Lee Chim Tuan and Lee Chim Huk were still on the active list of the S.V.C. Lee Chim Teck was no longer active. L
2 CONC ee Keng Kiat was buried in the family burial ground in Serangoon Road. His brothers are Lee Keng Yam, Lee Keng Yong, Lee Keng Liat and Lee Sek Long.
2 CONT
